  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe upcoming 7-day period is defined by a distinct two-phase \nevolution of the hemispheric upper-air pattern across North America: \nan initial fast, low-amplitude pseudo-zonal/progressive regime \nthrough Wednesday, followed by a major amplification into a deep \nEastern CONUS longwave trough late week.\n\n(Monday through Wednesday) An energetic Pacific jet stream will \nmaintain broad west-northwesterly to quasi-zonal flow across the \ncentral and eastern United States to start the work week. A \nshortwave trough passing north across Ontario on Monday will push a \nmoisture-starved cold front through lower Michigan, followed by \nbrief shortwave ridging into early Wednesday. Subsequent Pacific-\norigin shortwave impulses traversing the northern plains will build \n500mb heights aloft, reinforcing strong low-level warm air advection \n(WAA). As such, Tuesday/Wednesday could end up a bit warmer than \ncurrently forecast depending on the timing and track of upstream \nshortwaves traversing the Midwest. Excellent ensemble clustering \nyields high confidence for Tuesday, with expected high temperatures \nclimbing into the upper 50s to lower 60s supported by 925 mb \ntemperatures in the upper single digits to near +10C under westerly \nlow-level flow.\n\nModel guidance and ensemble members begin to diverge on the position \nand phasing of cyclogenesis developing over the Ohio Valley on \nWednesday and tracking into the Central Appalachians by Thursday. In \nfact, current solutions (e.g., 12z Canadian/UKMET/ICON/Euro) keep \nthe northern and southern stream branches separated longer, keeping \nthe primary low pressure and bulk of moisture suppressed through the \nsoutheastern United States. \n\n(Thursday night through Sunday) Strong cold air advection (CAA) \nbehind the departing surface system will scour out the mild Pacific \nairmass on Thursday. Southeast Michigan will reside firmly in the \ncore of the cyclonic upper-level trough by Friday. Steepened low- \nand mid-level lapse rates combined with thermal contrast (850 mb \ntemps dropping to -6C to -8C) over the warm Great Lakes waters will \nsetup robust lake-effect precipitation structures. This brings the \nfirst legitimate potential for lake-effect rain and snow showers \nacross the CWA. High temperatures will fall back into the 40s, \nslightly below Mid-November climatological normals.",
